2.  *An equivalence $\kappa:L_{K(1)}U_{\mathrm{mid}}\xrightarrow{\simeq}L_{K(1)}Y$, a rational map $r:L_0U_{\mathrm{mid}}\to L_0Y$, and a specified homotopy of $Q$-module maps between $$\label{eq:compatibility}
      \begin{gathered}
      L_0U_{\mathrm{mid}}\longrightarrow L_0L_{K(1)}U_{\mathrm{mid}}
              \xrightarrow{L_0\kappa}L_0L_{K(1)}Y,\\
      L_0U_{\mathrm{mid}}\xrightarrow rL_0Y\longrightarrow L_0L_{K(1)}Y.
      \end{gathered}$$

3.  *A map $b:V\to L_0X$ for which $$\label{eq:quotient-basis}
      t_0:V\xrightarrow bL_0X\xrightarrow{L_0q}L_0Y
                     \longrightarrow\mathop{\mathrm{cofib}}(r)$$ is an equivalence.

The support at the double origin in $N^2$ is a line in degree six, of scalar weight $-2$. The support in $D$ has cohomology in degrees three and five. In each degree it is a distribution module on $\mathbb P^1(\mathbb Q_p)$ with a one-dimensional continuous fiber. The scalar weights of these two fibers are respectively $-1$ and $-2$.*

*These descriptions are equivariant relative descriptions, with continuous profinite parameters. For the stabilizer of the direction $(1,0)$, written $$B=\left\{\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\0&d\end{pmatrix}
                \in\mathop{\mathrm{GL}}_2(\mathbb Z_p)\right\},$$ the two fibers have characters $\bar d^{-1}$ and $\bar a^{-1}\bar d^{-1}$. An element $v\in P_1=\mathbb Z_p^\times$ has characters $\bar v$ and $\bar v^2$, respectively. Normal Kummer twists are understood in these descriptions.

The inclusions induce a natural quasi-isomorphism $$\label{h3:co:decompletion-map}
 \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}_{U,e}C^\bullet_{\mathrm{cts}}(H,B_U^{1/p^e})
 \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }
 \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}_U C^\bullet_{\mathrm{cts}}(H,B_U^b).$$ It retains the commuting actions and remains a quasi-isomorphism after forming continuous families parametrized by any profinite space.

For clarity, the notation for algebraic localization always means $$\label{h3:ord:cochain-colimit}
 R\Gamma_{\mathrm{cts}}(G,B_0(\mathcal L))
   :=\mathop{\mathrm{colim}}_d C^\bullet_{\mathrm{cts}}(G,A_d(\mathcal L)).$$ An additional profinite parameter space $T$ means applying $C(T,-)$ at each displayed finite stage before taking the colimit. No topology on an unrestricted union of cochain values is being used.

The ordinary spectrum $L_1(T/p^\ell)$ is the totalization of the termwise $L_1(-/p^\ell)$ Morava descent object. Its terms are even, and their internal degree-zero cochain complex is the $D_\ell$ complex above. The corresponding spectral sequence is strongly convergent, has columns $0\leq s\leq5$, and has no differentials. All its abutment groups are finite in each stem. In particular there is a natural identification $$\label{h3:des:minus-three-edge}
 \pi_{-3}L_1(T/p^\ell)
       \cong H^3_{\mathrm{cts}}(G_3(k),D_\ell),$$ where the right side includes the coefficient Galois descent just described.

The groups $P_3$, $J_3$, and $\mathop{\mathrm{GL}}_2(\mathbb Z_p)$ have rational cohomology $H^1_{\mathrm b}=\mathbb Q_p$, $H^2_{\mathrm b}=0$, and $H^3_{\mathrm b}=\mathbb Q_p$. They admit nonzero classes $e_3$, $e_J$, and $e_2$, respectively, whose rational Lie-algebra images are nonzero multiples of the displayed trace form. For every compact open $K\subset J_3$, restriction followed by the compact Lie-algebra comparison identifies $H^*_{\mathrm b}(J_3)$ with $H^*(\mathfrak{gl}_3,\mathbb Q_p)$; the identifications for different $K$ agree under further restriction. For $$D=\left\{\begin{pmatrix}A&v\\0&a\end{pmatrix}:
 A\in\mathop{\mathrm{GL}}_2(\mathbb Z_p),\ a\in\mathbb Z_p^\times,\ v\in\mathbb Q_p^2\right\},$$ write $\iota:D\to J_3$ and $\rho:D\to\mathop{\mathrm{GL}}_2(\mathbb Z_p)$ for inclusion and projection. Then $$\label{h3:eq:rat-parabolic-trace}
 \iota^*e_J=b\rho^*e_2\quad\text{for some }b\in\mathbb Q_p^\times.$$

The first presentation in <a href="#h3:eq:rat-two-presentations" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h3:eq:rat-two-presentations">[h3:eq:rat-two-presentations]</a> has a natural arithmetic-equivariant splitting $$\label{h3:eq:rat-projective-h3}
 H^3_{\mathrm b}(\mathcal M)
   =H^3_{\mathrm b}(P_3)\oplus H^1_{\mathrm b}(P_3)(-1),$$ whose first inclusion is the classifying map $\operatorname{cl}_{c_{3,\mathcal M},\mathrm b}$ of [h3:rat:finite-classifying](#h3:rat:finite-classifying).

